{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat draft_reference_gaps.md && printf '\\\\n--- GAP JSON ---\\\\n' && cat draft_reference_gaps.json\"","aggregated_output":"# Post-draft citation-gap memo\n\n**Status:** ready\n\nOne medium-priority gap warrants targeted retrieval to contextualize the existing joint-maximum and coordinatewise Bonferroni comparators within multivariate conformal prediction. No broad literature expansion or manuscript restructuring is justified.\n\n## Grounded assessment\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, `references_provenance.json`, `claims_grounding.json`, `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. The memory summary was used only as qualitative context. All 25 distinct citation keys in the draft have bibliography and provenance entries; there are no unresolved citation keys. This checks coverage and traceability, not full-text verification of every cited claim.\n\nThe retained evidence and claims grounding identify the actual comparisons as scaled ILR, unscaled ILR, a joint simplex-coordinate maximum-residual region, and coordinatewise Bonferroni regions. They support the implementation and measured comparisons, but do not substitute for direct literature context for the multivariate comparator families.\n\n## Retrieval target\n\n- **Target ID:** `multivariate-rectangular-comparators`\n- **Existing section:** Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets\n- **Paragraph location:** Compared region constructions, `paper.tex:85`\n- **Priority:** medium\n- **Citation intent:** comparator\n- **Anchor:** “The latter is a simultaneous-coordinate baseline”\n- **Missing literature:** Multivariate conformal rectangular regions: joint maximum-residual calibration versus coordinatewise Bonferroni calibration.\n\nThe paragraph defines the simplex-coordinate maximum-residual and Bonferroni constructions without a direct comparator-family citation. Related Work discusses quantile-regression intervals, density-based adaptation, localized conformal inference, and compositional geometry, but does not explicitly connect these baselines to simultaneous vector-response prediction regions. A targeted pass for **one or two citations** would supply that context. This is an attribution and positioning gap, not a missing validity proof or evidence that the implemented baselines are incorrect. Medium priority reflects the draft’s already substantial support for general conformal theory.\n\n**Focused search topics:**\n\n1. Multivariate split conformal rectangular prediction regions using a maximum standardized absolute residual score.\n2. Multivariate conformal prediction contrasting joint maximum-residual calibration with coordinatewise Bonferroni intervals.\n\n**Bounded downstream edit:** Add a short comparator-context sentence or citation clause in this existing paragraph. Cite sources only for the general constructions they actually support; do not imply that the exact simplex-coordinate implementation was taken from a paper unless verified. Do not add unevaluated methods to the experimental comparison. If a suitable source is already in the bibliography, reuse it rather than adding a redundant reference. Stop once the direct comparator context is supported; no exact publication match is asserted at this stage.\n\n## Areas not requiring retrieval\n\n- **General calibration and adaptive scaling:** Related Work already cites split conformal, quantile regression, density-based adaptation, localization, and selection/aggregation. The existing `Lei2018` abstract also explicitly describes locally varying prediction-band lengths for heteroscedasticity; normalized-scale adaptation is not a separate retrieval target merely because a citation could be repeated in Method.\n- **Conditional and shifted validity:** `Vovk2012`, `Barber2021Limits`, `Tibshirani2019`, `Barber2023Beyond`, and `Gibbs2021` already ground the relevant distinctions. No shift-aware benchmark experiment is claimed.\n- **Compositional geometry and statistical ingredients:** Existing references cover compositional analysis, logistic-normal models, ILR coordinates, ridge regression, kernel smoothing, bootstrap uncertainty, and binomial intervals. The transformed-volume versus simplex-mass distinction is explained in the manuscript rather than presented as an unsupported novelty claim.\n- **Quantitative findings and implementation details:** Coverage, mass, utility, generator settings, and retained comparisons are grounded in the supplied data and claims artifacts. Additional literature citations cannot replace absent selector-only ablations, real-data experiments, alternative geometries, or structural-zero evidence; these remain stated limitations, not retrieval targets.\n\nNo reference retrieval, network lookup, or manuscript/bibliography/provenance modification was performed in this stage.\n\n--- GAP JSON ---\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"summary\": \"One medium-priority gap warrants targeted retrieval to contextualize the existing joint-maximum and coordinatewise Bonferroni comparators within multivariate conformal prediction.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [\n    \"Multivariate conformal rectangular regions: joint maximum-residual calibration versus coordinatewise Bonferroni calibration\"\n  ],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"multivariate-rectangular-comparators\",\n      \"section\": \"Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"comparator\",\n      \"reason\": \"The Compared region constructions paragraph defines the joint simplex-coordinate maximum-residual and coordinatewise Bonferroni baselines without directly citing multivariate prediction-region work. The existing related-work discussion cites adaptive intervals, general split conformal theory, and compositional geometry, but does not explicitly position these simultaneous vector-response constructions. One or two directly relevant citations would contextualize the comparator families without suggesting that the exact simplex implementations are published methods, changing the comparisons, or requiring new experiments.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"The latter is a simultaneous-coordinate baseline\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"multivariate split conformal rectangular prediction regions maximum standardized absolute residual score\",\n        \"multivariate conformal prediction joint maximum-residual calibration versus coordinatewise Bonferroni intervals\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"pwd; rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g 'reference_query_plan*' -g '*reference*plan*' -g 'memory.md'; printf '\\\\n--- INSTRUCTIONS ---\\\\n'; for path in /AGENTS.md /workspace/AGENTS.md /workspace/paper/AGENTS.md; do if [ -f \\\"\"'$path\" ]; then printf '\"'\\\\n%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$path\"; cat \"$path\"; fi; done; printf '\"'\\\\n--- MEMORY SKILL ---\\\\n'; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; printf '\\\\n--- PLAN SCHEMA CONTEXT ---\\\\n'; if [ -f reference_query_plan.json ]; then cat reference_query_plan.json; fi; printf '\\\\n--- DRAFT ---\\\\n'; sed -n '1,240p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"pwd; rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g 'reference_query_plan*' -g '*reference*plan*' -g 'memory.md'; printf '\\\\n--- INSTRUCTIONS ---\\\\n'; for path in /AGENTS.md /workspace/AGENTS.md /workspace/paper/AGENTS.md; do if [ -f \\\"\"'$path\" ]; then printf '\"'\\\\n%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$path\"; cat \"$path\"; fi; done; printf '\"'\\\\n--- MEMORY SKILL ---\\\\n'; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; printf '\\\\n--- PLAN SCHEMA CONTEXT ---\\\\n'; if [ -f reference_query_plan.json ]; then cat reference_query_plan.json; fi; printf '\\\\n--- DRAFT ---\\\\n'; sed -n '1,240p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"/workspace/paper\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261117_130005.log\nreference_query_plan.json\nreference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261117_130005.log\nreference_query_plan_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\nreference_query_plan.md\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\nmemory.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan.json\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n\n--- INSTRUCTIONS ---\n\n--- MEMORY SKILL ---\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n\n--- PLAN SCHEMA CONTEXT ---\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Reliability and efficiency of heteroscedastic split-conformal regions for observed positive continuous compositions, using fixed isometric-logratio coordinates and training-only local-scale selection under IID and covariate-shift synthetic regimes.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Split conformal with finite-sample order-statistic calibration and maximum standardized ILR residual scores\",\n    \"Fixed Helmert isometric-logratio representation with polynomial-ridge centers and log-residual base scales\",\n    \"Training-only cross-fitted residual-moment Gaussian-kernel smoothing with bounded shrink-to-neutral scalar scale corrections\",\n    \"Nested training-only swapped pseudo-calibration/evaluation splits selecting scales by conformal-rank ILR-box volume, with Gaussian-loss fallback\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Unscaled maximum-ILR-residual split-conformal regions sharing fitted centers\",\n    \"Simplex-coordinate L-infinity split-conformal regions sharing fitted centers\",\n    \"Bonferroni per-coordinate conformal intervals for simultaneous compositional coverage\",\n    \"Training-only constant-center and whole-simplex reference predictors\",\n    \"Weighted conformal prediction under covariate shift as a literature comparator only, not an evaluated method\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"Synthetic composition-v1: logistic-normal IID, heteroscedastic Dirichlet IID, and Dirichlet covariate-shift cases, each at K=3 and K=8 with six covariates\",\n    \"Completed final evaluation: eight statistical seeds, train/calibration/test sizes 1024/512/2048 per case, alpha=0.05/0.10/0.20\",\n    \"Feature-defined subgroup audits and normalized simplex-volume estimates using 128 contexts and 1024 shared uniform-simplex probes\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [\n    \"No neural architecture or coordinate-representation transition is evidenced. The strongest scale-only variants preserve polynomial-ridge centers, fixed Helmert ILR coordinates, and the region family; the supported procedural transition is from Gaussian-loss scale selection to training-only conformal-rank ILR-box-volume selection.\"\n  ],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"The rank-selected scale variant has the highest recorded search utility and a completed eight-seed final evaluation, but final evaluation of that variant alone does not establish a replicated selector-only causal advantage.\",\n    \"Final mean absolute coverage error is 0.013562011718750005 for IID cases versus 0.13034261067708333 for shifted cases; marginal calibration must not be advertised as conditional or covariate-shift validity.\",\n    \"Final aggregate utility exceeds the strongest aggregate comparator by 0.011594669497844157, yet mean simplex volume is 0.3034934467739529 versus 0.29170094596015084 for simplex L-infinity; there is no uniform volume-superiority claim.\",\n    \"ILR-box volume used for training selection and uniform-simplex reference volume used for evaluation are different measures under a nonlinear inverse transform.\",\n    \"Subgroup coverage, paired seed-block uncertainty, simultaneous baseline comparisons, and Monte Carlo resolution qualify efficiency and reliability conclusions; contexts and shared probes are not independent replicates.\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Lei G Sell Rinaldo Tibshirani Wasserman Distribution-Free Predictive Inference For Regression\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An exact starter-literature target anchors independent split calibration, finite-sample residual ranks, and the distinction between marginal coverage guarantees and realized coverage.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"locally adaptive conformal regression normalized residual scores heteroscedasticity\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The strongest variants learn feature-dependent residual scales and calibrate maximum standardized ILR residuals; this is the relevant adaptive-score literature, not a new neural architecture or a local calibration claim.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"conformal prediction model selection training split prediction region volume\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The selected implementation replaces Gaussian-loss scale selection with nested training-only pseudo-calibration ranks and ILR-box-volume selection while leaving actual calibration untouched. Search for selection and efficiency precedents without asserting novelty or a replicated selector effect.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"isometric logratio transformations compositional data analysis\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Fixed orthonormal Helmert ILR coordinates are the implemented response representation; foundational compositional geometry is necessary even though no representation transition was tested.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"logistic normal Dirichlet distributions compositional regression\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"These are the actual synthetic response families, with feature-dependent Dirichlet concentration. Distributional background is useful; internal case names, CIFAR-10, and unused real datasets would add no retrieval precision.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"multivariate conformal prediction rectangular regions Bonferroni simultaneous coverage\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The evaluation contrasts joint maximum-residual ILR boxes, simplex-coordinate L-infinity regions, and genuinely distinct Bonferroni per-coordinate intervals. Multivariate simultaneous-region literature provides the appropriate comparator context.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Tibshirani Barber Candes Ramdas Conformal Prediction Under Covariate Shift\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"This exact starter-literature target contextualizes substantially larger shifted coverage error and distinguishes ordinary unweighted split conformal from shift-aware weighting, which was not implemented in this run.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"conformal prediction marginal coverage conditional coverage limitations\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The artifacts report two feature-defined subgroup coverages but implement pooled calibration. Literature on marginal versus conditional validity prevents subgroup diagnostics from being mistaken for conditional guarantees or evaluated Mondrian methods.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"logratio inverse transformation simplex Jacobian volume measure\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Training minimizes an ILR-box-volume surrogate, whereas evaluation integrates membership against uniform-simplex probes. Change-of-variables background explains why efficiency in one measure need not imply smallest simplex volume; this is not evidence that the surrogate caused any observed result.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Any attempt to identify, infer, search for, or retrieve the prohibited paper, its authors, title, results, mirrors, summaries, code, or pages citing, discussing, or reproducing it\",\n    \"Broad joint-topic discovery searches such as conformal prediction compositional outputs evaluation or conformal uncertainty simplex benchmark that could directly target the prohibited study; search separate foundational neighborhoods instead\",\n    \"Citation-network expansion, cited-by searches, related-paper recommendations, or unrestricted survey crawling around potentially prohibited material\",\n    \"Queries treating classification labels, softmax probabilities, latent categorical probabilities, CIFAR-10, image classifiers, or text/topic datasets as evaluated responses or benchmarks\",\n    \"Queries presenting Hellinger, Euclidean-versus-ILR, additive-logratio, Mondrian, boundary-face mass, structural zeros, neural architectures, or architecture transfer as implemented comparisons\",\n    \"Queries presenting weighted conformal, jackknife+, conformalized quantile regression, credal sets, clustered conformal, or multivalid prediction as evaluated methods\",\n    \"Generic optimization, benchmarking, scientific-method, paper-writing, campaign chronology, or internal scoring and acceptance terminology without a specific grounded scientific purpose\",\n    \"Queries asserting distribution-free conditional validity, shift robustness, universal efficiency superiority, or a causal selector improvement not established by the recorded evidence\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n\n--- DRAFT ---\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Scale-Adaptive Conformal Regions:\\\\Reliability and Limits on the Simplex}\n\\author{}\n\\newcommand{\\R}{\\mathbb{R}}\n\\newcommand{\\simplex}{\\Delta^{K-1}_{\\circ}}\n\\newcommand{\\clip}{\\operatorname{clip}}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nPrediction regions for compositional responses must account for both the simplex constraint and variation in predictive uncertainty. We study whether scale-adaptive split-conformal regions in isometric-logratio coordinates improve reliability and efficiency together. A frozen training-only procedure combines ridge centers, local residual-moment scaling, and nested rank-based selection; independent calibration determines the final regions. Across logistic-normal, Dirichlet, and shifted-Dirichlet settings at two dimensions and eight held-out seeds, the results are condition-dependent. Mean absolute coverage error is 0.0136 under IID sampling but 0.1303 under shift. Aggregate utility exceeds the strongest standard comparator by 0.0116, with a descriptive paired 95\\% seed-bootstrap interval of [0.0047, 0.0179], yet mean uniform-simplex region mass is larger: 0.3035 versus 0.2917. Gains concentrate in higher-dimensional IID Dirichlet responses, whereas shifted comparisons favor the simplex-coordinate baseline. These findings support separating observed-response coverage, reference-measure efficiency, and distributional assumptions rather than interpreting an aggregate utility gain as uniform dominance or conditional validity.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/paired_overall/simplex_linf]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nCompositional predictions arise whenever the response is a nonnegative vector constrained to sum to one. A region around such a prediction should respect the constraint, cover the relevant future observation, and remain informative. These requirements are distinct. A small region in transformed coordinates need not occupy little of the simplex; a marginally calibrated region need not be reliable within a feature-defined population; and reliable IID behavior need not persist after a change in covariate distribution. The target itself also matters: uncertainty for an observed continuous composition is not the same problem as a set of possible class labels or a confidence region for an unobserved probability vector.\n\nSplit conformal prediction supplies a natural starting point because its marginal coverage guarantee separates fitting a predictive score from calibrating its threshold \\citep{Papadopoulos2002,Lei2018,Angelopoulos2021}. Compositional geometry nevertheless leaves important evaluation choices. Logratio representations remove the sum constraint but change how region size is measured \\citep{Aitchison1982,Egozcue2003}. Heteroscedastic scaling can redistribute widths without altering the calibration rule. Consequently, judging a region construction requires more than a single coverage number or a single transformed-space volume.\n\nWe ask: \\emph{how do training-only scale-adaptive logratio regions trade observed-composition coverage against uniform-simplex reference mass, relative to alternatives sharing the same fitted center?} We evaluate one fixed construction rather than claiming a comprehensive comparison of conformal methods. Its scale correction uses local moments of cross-fitted standardized residuals, with a neutral option and nested training-only selection. The final calibration sample is not used to choose that correction. The comparison includes unscaled logratio regions, a joint simplex-coordinate maximum-residual region, and coordinatewise Bonferroni regions.\n\nThe empirical answer is not uniform improvement. The scale-adaptive construction has higher aggregate utility but larger aggregate simplex mass than the simplex-coordinate baseline. The most favorable contrast occurs for IID Dirichlet responses in the larger dimension; shifted conditions provide clear adverse comparisons. Thus the useful contribution is a bounded evaluation of a specified configuration and its failure modes, not a new distribution-free guarantee. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json]]\n\nOur contributions are: (i) an inspectable training-only scale-adaptation specification with independent finite-rank calibration; (ii) a paired synthetic assessment separating coverage, reference mass, and their utility summary; and (iii) a seed-level uncertainty analysis that retains unfavorable condition-specific comparisons and distinguishes scaling effects from untested selector-specific explanations. The scope is strictly positive continuous responses under the stated generators. Structural zeros, latent probability recovery, real-data performance, and universal geometric preferences are outside the evidence.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=.98\\textwidth,height=1.6in,keepaspectratio]{figures/composition_reliability_overview.png}\n\\caption{Study overview. Training data determine centers and local scales, including internal rank-based selection. A separate calibration sample determines conformal thresholds. Evaluation distinguishes membership of observed compositions from mass under a uniform-simplex reference distribution; neither estimand establishes conditional or shifted validity.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py]]\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Calibration and adaptation.}\nThe conformal framework provides finite-sample predictive coverage under exchangeability \\citep{Vovk2005}; inductive constructions separate model fitting and calibration \\citep{Papadopoulos2002}. Regression studies compare the statistical and computational behavior of full and split conformal inference \\citep{Lei2018}, while jackknife+ uses leave-one-out predictions to account for fitting variability \\citep{Barber2021Jackknife}. Here the calibration procedure is ordinary split conformal: internal cross-fitting estimates scale information but is not jackknife+ calibration. Locally adaptive intervals have been developed through quantile regression \\citep{Romano2019,Sesia2020}, conditional density estimation \\citep{Lei2014,Izbicki2020}, and localized conformal constructions \\citep{Guan2023}. These motivate adaptation without establishing that our particular scalar correction is optimal. Selection and aggregation raise a separate issue from calibrating one fixed score \\citep{Yang2024}; our fitting choices are confined to training data.\n\n\\paragraph{What coverage does not imply.}\nConditional validity requires care even for inductive predictors \\citep{Vovk2012}. Distribution-free conditional predictive inference has fundamental limits \\citep{Barber2021Limits}, so pooled coverage should not be promoted to a feature-conditional guarantee. Under covariate shift, weighted conformal methods use covariate likelihood ratios \\citep{Tibshirani2019}; broader departures from exchangeability require their own analysis \\citep{Barber2023Beyond}. Adaptive conformal inference instead studies an online coverage-frequency target \\citep{Gibbs2021}. These extensions are not implemented here. Our shifted evaluation is a stress test of a fixed, unweighted calibration rule, not a comparison with shift-aware methods.\n\n\\paragraph{Composition and statistical measurement.}\nCompositional analysis treats ratios and the simplex as central objects \\citep{Aitchison1982,Pawlowsky2015}; logistic-normal distributions provide transformed-normal models on that space \\citep{Aitchison1980}. Isometric logratio (ILR) coordinates give an orthonormal representation \\citep{Egozcue2003}, but orthonormality in logratio geometry does not preserve uniform-simplex mass. Our centers use ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970}, and the scale correction is a shrunk kernel regression of residual moments in the spirit of \\citet{Nadaraya1964}. Uncertainty reporting uses resampling across independent seed blocks \\citep{Efron1979,Davison1997} and distinguishes those intervals from within-sample Wilson coverage intervals \\citep{Brown2001}. The contribution is the resulting controlled audit, not a claim to originate any of these ingredients.\n\n\\section{Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets}\n\\label{sec:regions}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and calibration}\nLet $X\\in\\R^6$ and $Y\\in\\simplex=\\{y\\in(0,1)^K:\\sum_k y_k=1\\}$. We predict a region $C_\\alpha(X)$ for an independent \\emph{observed response} $Y$, at target coverage $1-\\alpha$. No class label or latent multinomial parameter is evaluated. A fixed Helmert matrix $B\\in\\R^{K\\times(K-1)}$ satisfies $B^\\top B=I$ and $B^\\top\\mathbf 1=0$; the representation is\n\\begin{equation}\n z(y)=B^\\top\\log y,\\qquad h(z)=\\operatorname{softmax}(Bz).\n\\end{equation}\nFitted center $\\widehat\\mu(x)\\in\\R^{K-1}$ and positive scales $\\widehat s_j(x)$ define the nonconformity score\n\\begin{equation}\n A(x,y)=\\max_{1\\leq j<K}\\frac{|z_j(y)-\\widehat\\mu_j(x)|}{\\widehat s_j(x)}.\n \\label{eq:score}\n\\end{equation}\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_basis]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\nGiven $m$ independent calibration scores, use the $r$th smallest score with $r=\\lceil(m+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, or $+\\infty$ if $r>m$. The region consists of positive compositions satisfying $A(x,y)\\leq q_\\alpha$. It is an axis-aligned box in the fixed ILR basis, mapped back to the simplex; it is not a full-covariance ellipsoid. The finite-rank rule, including its infinite-threshold case, is owned by the evaluation code rather than the fitted predictor. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#quantile]]\n\nConditional on the training data, exchangeable calibration and test scores give $\\Pr\\{Y\\in C_\\alpha(X)\\}\\geq1-\\alpha$ by the standard conformal rank argument \\citep{Lei2018}. Ties are handled conservatively by the non-strict membership inequality. This is a statement about a random calibration sample and future response, not a guarantee for every realized test set, every fixed feature vector, or every group. Training-only adaptation leaves this argument intact because the final calibration responses do not enter score selection. The argument does not apply unchanged to our shifted test sample.\n\n\\subsection{Reliability, mass, and utility}\nFor each fitted instance, empirical coverage $\\widehat c_\\alpha$ is the fraction of test responses inside the region. Reliability error is $e_\\alpha=|\\widehat c_\\alpha-(1-\\alpha)|$, treating overcoverage and undercoverage symmetrically. Efficiency is the normalized uniform-simplex reference mass\n\\begin{equation}\n M_\\alpha=\\mathbb E_{X_{\\rm test}}\\Pr_{V\\sim\\operatorname{Dirichlet}(\\mathbf1)}\n \\{V\\in C_\\alpha(X_{\\rm test})\\}.\n \\label{eq:mass}\n\\end{equation}\nThe implementation estimates this probability with shared probes and test contexts, not by integrating ILR-box volume. Thus $M_\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ is dimension-normalized simplex volume, not probability content under the response law. We use ``mass'' to keep the reference distribution explicit. Small mass alone does not establish informative calibrated prediction. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#generate_arrays]]\n\nThe supplied utility combines the two empirical quantities:\n\\begin{equation}\n U_\\alpha=(1-\\widehat M_\\alpha)\n \\max\\left\\{0,1-\\frac{e_\\alpha}{1-\\alpha}\\right\\}.\n \\label{eq:utility}\n\\end{equation}\nIt rewards small regions and penalizes coverage error but is not a coverage constraint. A method can improve $U_\\alpha$ by trading mass against error; its ordering need not agree with either component. We therefore report all components and treat utility as a supplied descriptive summary, not a proof of scientific superiority. Overall utility averages conditions and nominal levels equally. IID and shifted errors are averaged separately, after taking absolute deviations within each seed, condition, and level. Taking the absolute value after averaging coverage would produce a different estimand. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#aggregate]]\n\n\\subsection{Compared region constructions}\n\\textbf{Scaled ILR} uses Eq.~\\eqref{eq:score} with the fitted scales described below. \\textbf{Unscaled ILR} retains exactly the same center and basis but sets every scale to one; it removes the entire scaling package, not only the local correction. \\textbf{Simplex max} maps the common center through $h$ and calibrates $\\max_k|y_k-h_k(\\widehat\\mu(x))|$. \\textbf{Bonferroni} calibrates separate absolute residuals in each simplex coordinate at miscoverage $\\alpha/K$ and intersects the resulting intervals with the simplex. The latter is a simultaneous-coordinate baseline, not a collection of independently claimed marginal intervals. All thresholds use their own appropriate calibration residuals from the same calibration split. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\n\nThese comparisons hold center predictions fixed while changing the residual geometry or scaling. The unscaled comparison can support conclusions about the complete scaling construction within this evaluation. It cannot identify the effect of local smoothing, cross-fitting, shrinkage, or the selector individually. The simplex comparison additionally changes coordinates, so any observed contrast is a comparison between region constructions rather than a causal estimate for one isolated component.\n\n\\section{Method: Training-Only Scale Adaptation}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\\subsection{Rationale and base fits}\nThe design hypothesis is that feature-dependent residual variability makes constant-width ILR regions inefficient, and that a bounded, shrunk correction may allocate widths more usefully. The counter-hypothesis is equally important: a correction selected using a transformed-space size objective may not reduce simplex mass, especially under extrapolation. The study evaluates these possibilities through paired region comparisons, not through a claim of optimal scale estimation.\n\nThe design vector is $\\phi(x)=(1,x,x^{\\odot2},\\sin x,x_0x_1)$, with coordinatewise squares and sine terms. Ridge regression of $z(Y)$ on $\\phi(X)$ uses penalty 1. A second ridge fit, with penalty 10, predicts $\\log\\max(|z_j(Y)-\\widehat\\mu_j(X)|,0.02)$ from the same design. These base-scale residuals are in-sample; cross-fitting is used for the subsequent moment correction, not retroactively for the base log-residual fit. The intercept penalty is $10^{-8}$. Predicted centers are clipped to $[-100,100]$ and base scales to $[10^{-4},100]$. These are numerical bounds, not distributional assumptions. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_features]]\n\n\\subsection{Local residual moments and bounded shrinkage}\nDeterministic three-fold cross-fitting within training data yields held-out centers $\\widehat\\mu^{(-i)}$ and base scales $\\widehat s^{0,(-i)}$. For each training row define\n\\begin{equation}\n v_i=\\frac{1}{K-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{K-1}\n \\left(\\frac{z_j(Y_i)-\\widehat\\mu_j^{(-i)}(X_i)}{\\widehat s_j^{0,(-i)}(X_i)}\\right)^2.\n\\end{equation}\nTraining-only feature means and standard deviations standardize the covariates. With standardized distance $d_i(x)$, Gaussian weights $w_i(x)=\\exp\\{-d_i(x)^2/(2b^2)\\}$ yield\n\\begin{equation}\n R(x)=\\frac{\\sum_iw_i(x)v_i+\\lambda}{\\sum_iw_i(x)+\\lambda},\\qquad\n a(x)=\\exp\\!\\left\\{\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\log\\clip(R(x),1/4,4)\\right\\}.\n \\label{eq:correction}\n\\end{equation}\nThe final scales are $\\widehat s_j(x)=a(x)\\widehat s^0_j(x)$, with a common row-scalar further bounded to respect the allowed scale range. Shrinkage returns $R(x)$ toward one when kernel support is weak; $\\beta=0$ is the neutral correction. The scalar leaves relative ILR-coordinate scales unchanged at a given $x$. It does not learn new directions or correlations. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#build_oof_residual_table]]\n\n\\subsection{Nested rank-based selection}\nThe finite family uses $\\beta\\in\\{0,0.5,1\\}$, $b\\in\\{0.5\\sqrt6,\\sqrt6\\}$, and $\\lambda\\in\\{10,50\\}$. For each of three outer training folds, the remaining rows fit both the base model and an inner cross-fitted moment table. The held-out fold is split into two halves, used in both orientations as pseudo-calibration and evaluation data. Each pseudo-calibration half gives a finite-rank radius $q_{f,\\alpha}$ at the same three miscoverage levels used for reporting. The selector minimizes the evaluation-row-weighted, level-averaged objective\n\\begin{equation}\n L(\\theta)=\\sum_f\\frac{|E_f|}{n_{\\rm train}}\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal A|}\n \\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\mathcal A}\\log\\left[\n \\frac1{|E_f|}\\sum_{i\\in E_f}(2q_{f,\\alpha})^{K-1}\n \\prod_{j=1}^{K-1}\\widehat s_{f,j,\\theta}(X_i)\\right],\n \\label{eq:selection}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $f$ indexes both orientations and $\\mathcal A=\\{0.05,0.10,0.20\\}$. The logarithm is applied to arithmetic mean box volume, evaluated stably, not to each row before averaging. Ties favor smaller $\\beta$. The selected correction is refitted using a full training moment table. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_select_correction]]\n\nCrucially, Eq.~\\eqref{eq:selection} measures \\emph{ILR-coordinate box volume}. The nonlinear inverse map does not have a constant simplex-volume Jacobian, so this is not Eq.~\\eqref{eq:mass}. A uniform multiplicative rescaling of all scales cancels against its conformal radius in the absence of clipping; improvement must come from changing allocation across inputs or coordinates, not merely multiplying all widths by a constant. The supplement derives this algebraic check. Neither property establishes that the selected correction improves the final mass or coverage.\n\nUnavailable finite ranks, nonpositive radii, or nonfinite objectives trigger a training-only Gaussian-loss selector over the same scale family; very small samples use a neutral correction. These are source-defined fallbacks. The retained results do not record how often each fallback was used or which setting was selected in each fit. We do not infer those frequencies from aggregate performance. Independent final calibration remains unchanged in every branch. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_select_correction_gaussian]]\n\n\\section{Experimental Design and Uncertainty}\n\\label{sec:design}\n\\subsection{Synthetic conditions and held-out protocol}\nThe available evidence covers one continuous-composition task, with six conditions: logistic-normal IID, Dirichlet IID, and shifted Dirichlet, each at $K\\in\\{3,8\\}$. Every condition uses six covariates, 1,024 training observations, 512 calibration observations, and 2,048 test observations. The held-out seeds are 1009, 1013, 1019, 1021, 1031, 1033, 1039, and 1049, with $\\alpha\\in\\{0.05,0.10,0.20\\}$. Each seed is a joint repeated unit containing all conditions and nominal levels, not eighteen independent replications. All recorded condition evaluations are complete. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data_manifest.json]]\n\nFor IID samples, $X$ is uniform on $[-1.5,1.5]^6$. Let $W$ be the fixed sinusoidal coefficient matrix specified in the supplement, and define\n\\begin{equation}\n \\eta_j(x)=0.7\\sin((x^\\top W)_j)+0.3x_0\\cos j,\n \\qquad j=0,\\ldots,K-2.\n\\end{equation}\nLogistic-normal responses are $h(\\eta(X)+\\epsilon)$ with independent Gaussian coordinates of standard deviation 0.25. The Dirichlet construction uses parameter vector $\\tau(x)h(\\eta(x))$, with $\\tau(x)=4+35/(1+\\exp(2x_0))$. The generator draws gamma coordinates, floors them at $10^{-12}$, and closes the vector to sum to one. The evaluated law is this numerically floored distribution, not an unfloored boundary model. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#generate_arrays]]\n\nShifted conditions change only the test covariate law: coordinates are drawn from $N(0.8,1)$ and clipped to $[-2.5,3]$. Training and calibration remain IID uniform, and the conditional response recipe is unchanged. This changes both feature frequencies and support. In particular, part of the test distribution lies outside training support; ordinary likelihood-ratio weighting cannot by itself recover information there. The comparison therefore probes a demanding shift, not an overlap-controlled weighting scenario. Its mechanisms are not separately manipulated.\n\nThe evaluated scale-adaptive configuration is frozen for the held-out analysis. Development configuration scores are not pooled with held-out measurements, and we do not compare an original initializer without matched final evidence. The evaluator reports completed qualification and passing information/state controls. This establishes completion of its specified checks, not population superiority or shift validity. Missing selector-specific ablations remain missing evidence rather than measured failures. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/control_check_pass_rate]]\n\n\\subsection{Paired measurements and uncertainty}\nEvery method sees the same train/calibration/test data within a seed and condition. Mass uses 128 randomly selected test contexts and a shared set of 1,024 uniform-simplex probes. Pairing the contexts and probes reduces avoidable comparison noise, although it does not remove Monte Carlo error. One hit per context changes its mass estimate by $1/1024$. A zero-hit context has the reported one-sided 95\\% binomial upper bound $1-0.05^{1/1024}=0.00292$; zero hits do not establish zero region mass. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/methods/scaled_ilr/0]]\n\nThe retained evaluator supplies paired percentile bootstrap intervals for method differences, resampling the eight seeds with 20,000 draws. Its identical cross-seed uncertainty object is repeated inside seed wrappers; we deduplicate it rather than treating these copies as new data. Utility comparisons additionally include endpoints using tail probability $0.05/(2\\cdot54)$ for the 54 case-by-level-by-baseline contrasts. We retain these adjusted endpoints in the supplement, but finite-bootstrap extreme quantiles with few seed blocks are descriptive and not exact familywise certificates. Mass intervals have no corresponding multiplicity adjustment. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#uncertainty_by_case]]\n\nFor the coverage curves, overall utility intervals, and feature-group mean coverage, we derive additional descriptive percentile intervals by jointly resampling seed blocks, preserving all condition and level records within a draw. The saved analysis uses 20,000 draws and bootstrap seed 20261117. These are analyses of retained measurements, not additional experiments. Per-seed Wilson intervals characterize test-membership sampling for one fitted/calibrated instance; we neither average their endpoints nor substitute them for cross-seed uncertainty. No confidence interval here captures arbitrary generator changes or adaptation to the held-out findings. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json]]\n\n\\section{Reliability--Efficiency Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n\\subsection{Near-target IID behavior does not survive the shift}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:coverage} shows the scale-adaptive construction across all nominal levels. The IID curves lie close to their targets, but not every estimated deviation is negligible. At nominal coverage 0.90, logistic-normal coverage is 0.8965 for $K=3$ and 0.8986 for $K=8$; IID Dirichlet coverage is 0.8912 and 0.8951. At nominal 0.80, IID Dirichlet coverage for $K=8$ is 0.7850 with a descriptive interval [0.7773, 0.7927]. Thus the average IID error should not be read as exact calibration in every condition. The standard finite-sample guarantee is marginal over calibration randomness, not an equality for a finite collection of realized fits. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/coverage]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=.98\\textwidth]{figures/coverage_by_regime.pdf}\n\\caption{Observed-composition coverage for scaled ILR. Left: $K=3$; right: $K=8$. Whiskers are descriptive 95\\% seed-block bootstrap intervals derived from retained observations. The truncated vertical axis highlights departures from the dashed nominal-coverage reference. IID behavior is close to target; the shifted Dirichlet deficit is substantial at every level. Lines connect nominal levels, not time.}\n\\label{fig:coverage}\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/coverage]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe shifted deficit is larger and consistent across levels. At nominal 0.90, mean coverage falls to 0.7858 [0.7647, 0.8112] for $K=3$ and 0.7399 [0.7096, 0.7739] for $K=8$. At nominal 0.80, the corresponding means are 0.6880 and 0.6239. Mean absolute error across seed-condition-level records increases from 0.0136 in IID conditions to 0.1303 under shift. This is evidence against interpreting training-only scale adaptation as a shift-robust calibration procedure. It is not evidence that conformal prediction failed under its exchangeability assumptions: those assumptions are absent in this test regime. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/coverage]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n\n\\subsection{Aggregate utility and simplex mass favor different readings}\nTable~\\ref{tab:main} reports all standard comparators. Scaled ILR has mean utility 0.6618, compared with 0.6502 for simplex max, 0.6296 for Bonferroni, and 0.6047 for unscaled ILR. Its paired aggregate utility advantage over simplex max is 0.0116 [0.0047, 0.0179]. Nevertheless, its mean simplex mass is 0.3035 rather than 0.2917 for simplex max. The favorable utility contrast therefore does not mean the scale-adaptive regions are smaller on average. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/methods]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/paired_overall]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Held-out mean errors, normalized simplex mass, and utility. Errors are absolute deviations computed before averaging. Utility intervals are descriptive joint seed-block bootstrap intervals from our retained-data analysis. Smaller error and mass are favorable; larger utility is favorable.}\n\\label{tab:main}\n\\input{analysis/main_table.tex}\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/methods]]\n\\end{table}\n\nThe same table also prevents an overly favorable reliability interpretation. Both unscaled ILR and simplex max have slightly smaller aggregate IID errors than scaled ILR, whereas all three have larger shifted errors. Bonferroni has IID error 0.0204 and shifted error 0.1620. The scale-adaptive construction reduces aggregate mass relative to unscaled ILR, from 0.3382 to 0.3035, and improves utility by 0.0572 [0.0509, 0.0642]. This is evidence about the full scaling package at the common center, not a measured gain attributable specifically to rank selection. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/methods]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/paired_overall/unscaled_ilr]]\n\n\\subsection{Condition-specific comparisons rule out uniform dominance}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:paired} compares scaled ILR with simplex max. Positive utility differences and negative mass differences have different favorable directions, shown in separate columns. The largest favorable contrasts occur for IID Dirichlet responses at $K=8$. At $\\alpha=0.10$, utility increases by 0.1962 [0.1741, 0.2168], while mass decreases by 0.2024 with interval [$-0.2254$,$-0.1800$]. All reported nominal levels in that condition favor scaled ILR on both quantities. This localized pattern is stronger than the small aggregate utility difference, but it does not identify which part of the scaling construction is responsible. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/retained_uncertainty/DIR_IID_K8/paired_baselines/simplex_linf]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=.98\\textwidth]{figures/paired_region_tradeoffs.pdf}\n\\caption{Scaled ILR minus simplex max, with evaluator-supplied paired descriptive 95\\% seed-bootstrap intervals. Rows distinguish $K$; columns distinguish utility (positive favors scaled ILR) and uniform-simplex mass (negative means smaller). All nominal levels are shown. LN denotes logistic-normal; Dir denotes Dirichlet. Shifted conditions reverse the favorable IID Dirichlet pattern.}\n\\label{fig:paired}\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/retained_uncertainty]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe contrasts are not uniformly favorable even without shift. For IID Dirichlet $K=3$ at $\\alpha=0.20$, utility is lower by 0.0323 [$-0.0451$,$-0.0199$], and mass is larger by 0.0261. Several logistic-normal contrasts are small, with descriptive intervals including zero. These results reject a blanket preference for scaled ILR across response laws, dimensions, and target levels. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/retained_uncertainty/DIR_IID_K3/paired_baselines/simplex_linf]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/retained_uncertainty/LN_IID_K8/paired_baselines/simplex_linf]]\n\nEvery shifted utility contrast against simplex max is negative, and every shifted mass contrast is positive. At $\\alpha=0.10$, utility differences are $-0.0732$ [$-0.0858$,$-0.0591$] for $K=3$ and $-0.0765$ [$-0.1032$,$-0.0492$] for $K=8$. Corresponding mass increases are 0.1281 and 0.1639. Importantly, scaled ILR still has the smaller aggregate shifted coverage error: its adverse shifted utility comparison cannot be summarized as worse coverage alone. The larger regions offset that reliability advantage under the supplied utility. % @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/retained_uncertainty/DIR_SHIFT_K3/paired_baselines/simplex_linf]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/retained_uncertainty/DIR_SHIFT_K8/paired_baselines/simplex_linf]]\n\n\\subsection{Controls and what they identify}\nThe evaluator checks reset behavior, constant inputs, permuted inputs, and a zero-radius point-copy control. All recorded checks pass. Constant-input and permuted-input comparisons disrupt access to predictive features at query time; they are information controls, not training-time component removals. Likewise, whole-simplex and zero-radius endpoints check the meaning of mass and coverage but cannot recommend a useful predictive region. Feature-group coverage by the sign of $x_0$ is reported in the supplement to expose heterogeneity without claiming groupwise calibration. No group-specific threshold is fitted. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/control_check_pass_rate]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the positive comparisons establish.}\nAt the evaluated center and data budget, the scaling package can improve utility and reduce simplex mass relative to removing scaling. It also provides large paired gains against simplex max in the higher-dimensional IID Dirichlet condition. These are valid configuration-level comparisons, with matched data and reported seed variation. They are not evidence that every constituent is needed, nor that the rank-based objective is preferable to Gaussian-loss selection. The final measurements contain no matched selector-only comparison. Development configurations change multiple choices and use a different evidence split, so their scores cannot fill that gap.\n\n\\paragraph{Why the negative comparisons matter.}\nThe mismatch between the selection objective and the evaluation reference measure is an explicit limitation of the construction. The result is compatible with that mismatch, but does not isolate it as the cause of unfavorable mass differences. The shift also jointly changes support, feature frequencies, and the frequency of high-variability responses. Its adverse coverage cannot be attributed solely to extrapolation, scale misspecification, or one design choice without additional controlled evidence. Standard exchangeability theory explains why no unchanged guarantee is available, not the magnitude of the observed deficit.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty and selection scope.}\nOnly the retained seed blocks quantify repetition. The audit framing is retrospective; its intervals do not adjust for choosing the manuscript's emphasis. Paired resampling respects dependence across conditions and nominal levels, but few independent blocks limit precision, especially at multiplicity-adjusted tails. Conditional-on-fit Wilson intervals, Monte Carlo mass diagnostics, and seed-bootstrap intervals answer different questions. Shared probes introduce dependence across contexts, so an average of per-context standard errors is not a confidence interval for the overall mass. The main comparisons and the overall utility gap are descriptive; no claim is made that they remain significant after arbitrary selection of endpoints or across unobserved tasks.\n\n\\paragraph{Domain and geometric scope.}\nThe study has no public-data experiment, alternative ILR basis, learned covariance region, structural-zero response, or independent boundary-face reference measure. Positivity flooring makes logratios computable but is not a principled solution for true zeros. Uniform-simplex mass is a clearly defined reference measure, not a universal notion of user-relevant uncertainty. Its dimension dependence and finite probe resolution can hide small or boundary-concentrated regions. Conversely, a smaller mass need not imply better prediction for the actual response law. These limitations prevent conclusions about classification label sets, latent probability regions, or general deployment reliability.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical interpretation.}\nThe evidence recommends an evaluation discipline rather than a universal method choice: specify the response being covered, state the reference measure, compare matched configurations, retain condition-specific failures, and distinguish marginal from conditional and shifted validity. Aggregate utility can be useful for summarization only after its components are visible. Where support changes or calibration is reused for selection, assumptions must be reassessed rather than inferred from an apparently favorable score. Neither issue is repaired by a publication decision or by satisfying a completion check.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nScale-adaptive conformal regions for continuous compositions exhibit a measurable but bounded reliability--efficiency trade-off. In the evaluated synthetic settings, independent split calibration gives near-target IID behavior, and the complete scaling construction improves aggregate utility relative to the unscaled matched-center alternative. Against the simplex-coordinate maximum-residual baseline, however, the positive aggregate utility difference coexists with larger overall simplex mass, favorable higher-dimensional IID Dirichlet comparisons, and adverse shifted comparisons. No uniformly superior region construction emerges from these measurements.\n\nThe distinction between training-time ILR-box volume and evaluation-time uniform-simplex mass is therefore essential to interpreting the method. Equally essential is the distinction between IID calibration and shifted reliability: training-only adaptation preserves the standard calibration argument but does not make it shift-invariant. Further evidence would be needed to isolate the selector's contribution, assess support-preserving shifts, or study structural zeros and real responses. Within the available evidence, the defensible conclusion is that coverage, geometric efficiency, and assumptions must be audited separately, with configuration-level gains stated at their measured scope.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{iclr2026_conference}\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\\label{sec:supplement}\n\n\\subsection{Detailed selection specification and algebraic checks}\nThe archived implementation is \\texttt{source\\_evidence/selected\\_model.py}; the evaluated entrypoint declaration is retained in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/selected\\_spec.toml}. Basis and design construction appear at lines 89--107; base fitting and moment tables at lines 114--152; smoothing and bounded scale correction at lines 154--185; Gaussian-loss fallback at lines 188--234; and rank-based selection at lines 237--352. These readable implementations, rather than a hash or a previous assessment, support the concrete method description. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py]]\n\nEach call to the deterministic fold constructor permutes the relevant training indices with NumPy random seed 0 and splits them into three parts. Feature standard deviations in moment tables are bounded below by $10^{-8}$, standardized residual scales by $10^{-4}$, and kernel queries are processed in batches of 128. Neutral settings share predictions even when their redundant bandwidth or prior parameters differ. The method caches moment-ratio evaluations for repeated bandwidth/prior pairs. The selector's outer pseudo-calibration halves and evaluation halves are disjoint from one another and from the rows fitting their models. Internal folds overlap across fits and must not be counted as independent scientific replications. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_training_folds]]\n\nTo check global-scale invariance, replace $s(x)$ by $c\\,s(x)$ for constant $c>0$, ignoring numerical clipping. Every calibration score is divided by $c$, so its order-statistic radius becomes $q_\\alpha/c$. The resulting coordinate half-width is $(q_\\alpha/c)(c\\,s_j(x))=q_\\alpha s_j(x)$. Its ILR-box volume is unchanged:\n\\begin{equation}\n (2q_\\alpha/c)^{K-1}\\prod_{j=1}^{K-1}c\\,s_j(x)\n =(2q_\\alpha)^{K-1}\\prod_{j=1}^{K-1}s_j(x).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
