error\": 0.07864583333333335}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=final | scope=idea\n  - summary: Wrote Training-Only Conformal-Rank Scale Selection and matching rubric bundles; status=idea_bundle_written, new method unexecuted. Isolate the selector while preserving accepted centers and scale family. gate=updated; trigger=b; reason=The recorded accepted scale-only successor supplies the first evidence-backed promising method category, while its tiny unreplicated aggregate gain supports neither causal attribution nor a shift-coverage claim.\n  - evidence: Historical cycle 02 passed: combined_score=0.6421289430111841, correct=true, control_check_pass_rate=1.0, iid_coverage_error=0.018196614583333326, shift_coverage_error=0.07864583333333335, mean_volume=0.3331370883517795; prior incumbent recorded as 0.6417212587320733. Named results-relative incumbent source exposes training-only X/Y and Gaussian-loss scale selection; no evaluator inspection. Rejected tail-model/coordinate-shape changes for attribution and Mondrian controls as unsupported. Required bundles statically reviewed with matching rubric content; 4/5/7/5 literal implementation snippets. Sole cycle-local memory target is ideas/03_training_only_conformal_rank_scale_selection/memory.md. No new scientific computation or computational validation.\n- 2026-11-17T12:53:43.300362+00:00 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: generated idea bundle (auto-healed by idea.sh)\n  - evidence: idea_bundle.json + target_rubrics.json\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T12:57:27.030079+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.6450635814905741, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"baseline_utility_gap\": 0.016801862721265826, \"combined_score\": 0.6450635814905741, \"control_check_pass_rate\": 1.0, \"correct\": true, \"iid_coverage_error\": 0.018120659722222213, \"mean_volume\": 0.3222492359302662, \"shift_coverage_error\": 0.11304253472222224}\n\n--- DRAFT TARGET ---\n    35\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py]]\n    36\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py]]\n    37\t\\end{figure}\n    38\t\n    39\t\\section{Related Work}\n    40\t\\paragraph{Calibration and adaptation.}\n    41\tThe conformal framework provides finite-sample predictive coverage under exchangeability \\citep{Vovk2005}; inductive constructions separate model fitting and calibration \\citep{Papadopoulos2002}. Regression studies compare the statistical and computational behavior of full and split conformal inference \\citep{Lei2018}, while jackknife+ uses leave-one-out predictions to account for fitting variability \\citep{Barber2021Jackknife}. Here the calibration procedure is ordinary split conformal: internal cross-fitting estimates scale information but is not jackknife+ calibration. Locally adaptive intervals have been developed through quantile regression \\citep{Romano2019,Sesia2020}, conditional density estimation \\citep{Lei2014,Izbicki2020}, and localized conformal constructions \\citep{Guan2023}. These motivate adaptation without establishing that our particular scalar correction is optimal. Selection and aggregation raise a separate issue from calibrating one fixed score \\citep{Yang2024}; our fitting choices are confined to training data.\n    42\t\n    43\t\\paragraph{What coverage does not imply.}\n    44\tConditional validity requires care even for inductive predictors \\citep{Vovk2012}. Distribution-free conditional predictive inference has fundamental limits \\citep{Barber2021Limits}, so pooled coverage should not be promoted to a feature-conditional guarantee. Under covariate shift, weighted conformal methods use covariate likelihood ratios \\citep{Tibshirani2019}; broader departures from exchangeability require their own analysis \\citep{Barber2023Beyond}. Adaptive conformal inference instead studies an online coverage-frequency target \\citep{Gibbs2021}. These extensions are not implemented here. Our shifted evaluation is a stress test of a fixed, unweighted calibration rule, not a comparison with shift-aware methods.\n    45\t\n    46\t\\paragraph{Composition and statistical measurement.}\n    47\tCompositional analysis treats ratios and the simplex as central objects \\citep{Aitchison1982,Pawlowsky2015}; logistic-normal distributions provide transformed-normal models on that space \\citep{Aitchison1980}. Isometric logratio (ILR) coordinates give an orthonormal representation \\citep{Egozcue2003}, but orthonormality in logratio geometry does not preserve uniform-simplex mass. Our centers use ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970}, and the scale correction is a shrunk kernel regression of residual moments in the spirit of \\citet{Nadaraya1964}. Uncertainty reporting uses resampling across independent seed blocks \\citep{Efron1979,Davison1997} and distinguishes those intervals from within-sample Wilson coverage intervals \\citep{Brown2001}. The contribution is the resulting controlled audit, not a claim to originate any of these ingredients.\n    48\t\n    49\t\\section{Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets}\n    50\t\\label{sec:regions}\n    51\t\\subsection{Problem setup and calibration}\n    52\tLet $X\\in\\R^6$ and $Y\\in\\simplex=\\{y\\in(0,1)^K:\\sum_k y_k=1\\}$. We predict a region $C_\\alpha(X)$ for an independent \\emph{observed response} $Y$, at target coverage $1-\\alpha$. No class label or latent multinomial parameter is evaluated. A fixed Helmert matrix $B\\in\\R^{K\\times(K-1)}$ satisfies $B^\\top B=I$ and $B^\\top\\mathbf 1=0$; the representation is\n    53\t\\begin{equation}\n    54\t z(y)=B^\\top\\log y,\\qquad h(z)=\\operatorname{softmax}(Bz).\n    55\t\\end{equation}\n    56\tFitted center $\\widehat\\mu(x)\\in\\R^{K-1}$ and positive scales $\\widehat s_j(x)$ define the nonconformity score\n    57\t\\begin{equation}\n    58\t A(x,y)=\\max_{1\\leq j<K}\\frac{|z_j(y)-\\widehat\\mu_j(x)|}{\\widehat s_j(x)}.\n    59\t \\label{eq:score}\n    60\t\\end{equation}\n    61\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_basis]]\n    62\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\n    63\tGiven $m$ independent calibration scores, use the $r$th smallest score with $r=\\lceil(m+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$, or $+\\infty$ if $r>m$. The region consists of positive compositions satisfying $A(x,y)\\leq q_\\alpha$. It is an axis-aligned box in the fixed ILR basis, mapped back to the simplex; it is not a full-covariance ellipsoid. The finite-rank rule, including its infinite-threshold case, is owned by the evaluation code rather than the fitted predictor. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#quantile]]\n    64\t\n    65\tConditional on the training data, exchangeable calibration and test scores give $\\Pr\\{Y\\in C_\\alpha(X)\\}\\geq1-\\alpha$ by the standard conformal rank argument \\citep{Lei2018}. Ties are handled conservatively by the non-strict membership inequality. This is a statement about a random calibration sample and future response, not a guarantee for every realized test set, every fixed feature vector, or every group. Training-only adaptation leaves this argument intact because the final calibration responses do not enter score selection. The argument does not apply unchanged to our shifted test sample.\n    66\t\n    67\t\\subsection{Reliability, mass, and utility}\n    68\tFor each fitted instance, empirical coverage $\\widehat c_\\alpha$ is the fraction of test responses inside the region. Reliability error is $e_\\alpha=|\\widehat c_\\alpha-(1-\\alpha)|$, treating overcoverage and undercoverage symmetrically. Efficiency is the normalized uniform-simplex reference mass\n    69\t\\begin{equation}\n    70\t M_\\alpha=\\mathbb E_{X_{\\rm test}}\\Pr_{V\\sim\\operatorname{Dirichlet}(\\mathbf1)}\n    71\t \\{V\\in C_\\alpha(X_{\\rm test})\\}.\n    72\t \\label{eq:mass}\n    73\t\\end{equation}\n    74\tThe implementation estimates this probability with shared probes and test contexts, not by integrating ILR-box volume. Thus $M_\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ is dimension-normalized simplex volume, not probability content under the response law. We use ``mass'' to keep the reference distribution explicit. Small mass alone does not establish informative calibrated prediction. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#generate_arrays]]\n    75\t\n    76\tThe supplied utility combines the two empirical quantities:\n    77\t\\begin{equation}\n    78\t U_\\alpha=(1-\\widehat M_\\alpha)\n    79\t \\max\\left\\{0,1-\\frac{e_\\alpha}{1-\\alpha}\\right\\}.\n    80\t \\label{eq:utility}\n    81\t\\end{equation}\n    82\tIt rewards small regions and penalizes coverage error but is not a coverage constraint. A method can improve $U_\\alpha$ by trading mass against error; its ordering need not agree with either component. We therefore report all components and treat utility as a supplied descriptive summary, not a proof of scientific superiority. Overall utility averages conditions and nominal levels equally. IID and shifted errors are averaged separately, after taking absolute deviations within each seed, condition, and level. Taking the absolute value after averaging coverage would produce a different estimand. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#aggregate]]\n    83\t\n    84\t\\subsection{Compared region constructions}\n    85\t\\textbf{Scaled ILR} uses Eq.~\\eqref{eq:score} with the fitted scales described below. \\textbf{Unscaled ILR} retains exactly the same center and basis but sets every scale to one; it removes the entire scaling package, not only the local correction. \\textbf{Simplex max} maps the common center through $h$ and calibrates $\\max_k|y_k-h_k(\\widehat\\mu(x))|$. \\textbf{Bonferroni} calibrates separate absolute residuals in each simplex coordinate at miscoverage $\\alpha/K$ and intersects the resulting intervals with the simplex. The latter is a simultaneous-coordinate baseline, not a collection of independently claimed marginal intervals. All thresholds use their own appropriate calibration residuals from the same calibration split. % @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#method_records]]\n    86\t\n    87\tThese comparisons hold center predictions fixed while changing the residual geometry or scaling. The unscaled comparison can support conclusions about the complete scaling construction within this evaluation. It cannot identify the effect of local smoothing, cross-fitting, shrinkage, or the selector individually. The simplex comparison additionally changes coordinates, so any observed contrast is a comparison between region constructions rather than a causal estimate for one isolated component.\n    88\t\n    89\t\\section{Method: Training-Only Scale Adaptation}\n    90\t\\label{sec:method}\n    91\t\\subsection{Rationale and base fits}\n    92\tThe design hypothesis is that feature-dependent residual variability makes constant-width ILR regions inefficient, and that a bounded, shrunk correction may allocate widths more usefully. The counter-hypothesis is equally important: a correction selected using a transformed-space size objective may not reduce simplex mass, especially under extrapolation. The study evaluates these possibilities through paired region comparisons, not through a claim of optimal scale estimation.\n    93\t\n    94\tThe design vector is $\\phi(x)=(1,x,x^{\\odot2},\\sin x,x_0x_1)$, with coordinatewise squares and sine terms. Ridge regression of $z(Y)$ on $\\phi(X)$ uses penalty 1. A second ridge fit, with penalty 10, predicts $\\log\\max(|z_j(Y)-\\widehat\\mu_j(X)|,0.02)$ from the same design. These base-scale residuals are in-sample; cross-fitting is used for the subsequent moment correction, not retroactively for the base log-residual fit. The intercept penalty is $10^{-8}$. Predicted centers are clipped to $[-100,100]$ and base scales to $[10^{-4},100]$. These are numerical bounds, not distributional assumptions. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#_features]]\n    95\t\n    96\t\\subsection{Local residual moments and bounded shrinkage}\n    97\tDeterministic three-fold cross-fitting within training data yields held-out centers $\\widehat\\mu^{(-i)}$ and base scales $\\widehat s^{0,(-i)}$. For each training row define\n    98\t\\begin{equation}\n    99\t v_i=\\frac{1}{K-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{K-1}\n   100\t \\left(\\frac{z_j(Y_i)-\\widehat\\mu_j^{(-i)}(X_i)}{\\widehat s_j^{0,(-i)}(X_i)}\\right)^2.\n   101\t\\end{equation}\n   102\tTraining-only feature means and standard deviations standardize the covariates. With standardized distance $d_i(x)$, Gaussian weights $w_i(x)=\\exp\\{-d_i(x)^2/(2b^2)\\}$ yield\n   103\t\\begin{equation}\n   104\t R(x)=\\frac{\\sum_iw_i(x)v_i+\\lambda}{\\sum_iw_i(x)+\\lambda},\\qquad\n   105\t a(x)=\\exp\\!\\left\\{\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\log\\clip(R(x),1/4,4)\\right\\}.\n   106\t \\label{eq:correction}\n   107\t\\end{equation}\n   108\tThe final scales are $\\widehat s_j(x)=a(x)\\widehat s^0_j(x)$, with a common row-scalar further bounded to respect the allowed scale range. Shrinkage returns $R(x)$ toward one when kernel support is weak; $\\beta=0$ is the neutral correction. The scalar leaves relative ILR-coordinate scales unchanged at a given $x$. It does not learn new directions or correlations. % @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py#build_oof_residual_table]]\n   109\t\n   110\t\\subsection{Nested rank-based selection}\n\n--- MEMORY INSTRUCTIONS END ---\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n\n--- EXISTING CONCLUSIONS ---\ncat: conclusions.md: No such file or directory\n\n--- STATUS ---\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
