{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"printf '\\\\n--- RELATED WORK ---\\\\n'; sed -n '35,52p' paper.tex; printf '\\\\n--- EXISTING GENERAL-CONFORMAL PROVENANCE ---\\\\n'; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nprovenance=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nfor record in provenance['references']:\n if record['citation_key'] in {'Vovk2005','Papadopoulos2002','Lei2018','Angelopoulos2021','Romano2019','Sesia2020'}:\n  print(record['citation_key'], '|', record['title'], '|', record['read_scope'])\nPY\nprintf '\\\\n--- CURRENT DISTILLED CONCLUSIONS ---\\\\n'; cat global_memory/conclusions.md; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY END ---\\\\n'; tail -n 16 memory.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"printf '\\\\n--- RELATED WORK ---\\\\n'; sed -n '35,52p' paper.tex; printf '\\\\n--- EXISTING GENERAL-CONFORMAL PROVENANCE ---\\\\n'; python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nprovenance=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nfor record in provenance['references']:\n if record['citation_key'] in {'Vovk2005','Papadopoulos2002','Lei2018','Angelopoulos2021','Romano2019','Sesia2020'}:\n  print(record['citation_key'], '|', record['title'], '|', record['read_scope'])\nPY\nprintf '\\\\n--- CURRENT DISTILLED CONCLUSIONS ---\\\\n'; cat global_memory/conclusions.md; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY END ---\\\\n'; tail -n 16 memory.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n--- RELATED WORK ---\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected_model.py]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py]]\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Calibration and adaptation.}\nThe conformal framework provides finite-sample predictive coverage under exchangeability \\citep{Vovk2005}; inductive constructions separate model fitting and calibration \\citep{Papadopoulos2002}. Regression studies compare the statistical and computational behavior of full and split conformal inference \\citep{Lei2018}, while jackknife+ uses leave-one-out predictions to account for fitting variability \\citep{Barber2021Jackknife}. Here the calibration procedure is ordinary split conformal: internal cross-fitting estimates scale information but is not jackknife+ calibration. Locally adaptive intervals have been developed through quantile regression \\citep{Romano2019,Sesia2020}, conditional density estimation \\citep{Lei2014,Izbicki2020}, and localized conformal constructions \\citep{Guan2023}. These motivate adaptation without establishing that our particular scalar correction is optimal. Selection and aggregation raise a separate issue from calibrating one fixed score \\citep{Yang2024}; our fitting choices are confined to training data.\n\n\\paragraph{What coverage does not imply.}\nConditional validity requires care even for inductive predictors \\citep{Vovk2012}. Distribution-free conditional predictive inference has fundamental limits \\citep{Barber2021Limits}, so pooled coverage should not be promoted to a feature-conditional guarantee. Under covariate shift, weighted conformal methods use covariate likelihood ratios \\citep{Tibshirani2019}; broader departures from exchangeability require their own analysis \\citep{Barber2023Beyond}. Adaptive conformal inference instead studies an online coverage-frequency target \\citep{Gibbs2021}. These extensions are not implemented here. Our shifted evaluation is a stress test of a fixed, unweighted calibration rule, not a comparison with shift-aware methods.\n\n\\paragraph{Composition and statistical measurement.}\nCompositional analysis treats ratios and the simplex as central objects \\citep{Aitchison1982,Pawlowsky2015}; logistic-normal distributions provide transformed-normal models on that space \\citep{Aitchison1980}. Isometric logratio (ILR) coordinates give an orthonormal representation \\citep{Egozcue2003}, but orthonormality in logratio geometry does not preserve uniform-simplex mass. Our centers use ridge regression \\citep{Hoerl1970}, and the scale correction is a shrunk kernel regression of residual moments in the spirit of \\citet{Nadaraya1964}. Uncertainty reporting uses resampling across independent seed blocks \\citep{Efron1979,Davison1997} and distinguishes those intervals from within-sample Wilson coverage intervals \\citep{Brown2001}. The contribution is the resulting controlled audit, not a claim to originate any of these ingredients.\n\n\\section{Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets}\n\\label{sec:regions}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and calibration}\nLet $X\\in\\R^6$ and $Y\\in\\simplex=\\{y\\in(0,1)^K:\\sum_k y_k=1\\}$. We predict a region $C_\\alpha(X)$ for an independent \\emph{observed response} $Y$, at target coverage $1-\\alpha$. No class label or latent multinomial parameter is evaluated. A fixed Helmert matrix $B\\in\\R^{K\\times(K-1)}$ satisfies $B^\\top B=I$ and $B^\\top\\mathbf 1=0$; the representation is\n\n--- EXISTING GENERAL-CONFORMAL PROVENANCE ---\nVovk2005 | Algorithmic Learning in a Random World | Publisher abstract or book overview read; saved HTML. No references or related-article links followed.\nPapadopoulos2002 | Inductive Confidence Machines for Regression | Publisher abstract or book overview read; saved HTML. No references or related-article links followed.\nLei2018 | Distribution-Free Predictive Inference For Regression | Title, authors, abstract only; no references or citation links followed.\nAngelopoulos2021 | A Gentle Introduction to Conformal Prediction and Distribution-Free Uncertainty Quantification | Title, authors, abstract only; no references or citation links followed.\nRomano2019 | Conformalized Quantile Regression | Title, authors, abstract only; no references or citation links followed.\nSesia2020 | A comparison of some conformal quantile regression methods | Title, authors, abstract only; no references or citation links followed.\n\n--- CURRENT DISTILLED CONCLUSIONS ---\nPrioritize training-only, shrink-to-neutral local scale corrections with fixed ILR centers as a promising candidate family, but require independent replication before interpreting a small accepted aggregate improvement as a robust scientific advantage. Keep selector-only comparisons within the accepted family to separate model effects from surrogate-choice effects; conformal-rank efficiency selection remains an untested next experiment. Preserve evaluator-owned calibration and the fixed prediction interface, distinguish ILR-box efficiency from simplex volume, and do not infer conditional or covariate-shift coverage guarantees from marginal acceptance. Treat previous lower-scoring accepted-control runs as scientific underperformance with unresolved mechanism, not established protocol defects.\n\n--- MEMORY END ---\n\n### 2026-11-17T13:48:01.343998+00:00 | step=verify | scope=paper\n- summary: Draft and retained-data plots written; 96 mapped claims pass grounding and 25 consulted references pass citation checks; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Derived descriptive intervals refine reporting without establishing a new method recommendation.\n- evidence: IID/shift coverage errors remain .013562/.130343. Paired aggregate simplex-baseline utility difference .011595 with descriptive seed-bootstrap [.004738,.017943] is reported alongside larger mass .303493 versus .291701 and every unfavorable shifted contrast. Rejected unrelated bibliography hits, wrong DOI matches and selector-causal framing. Scientific verifier currently has zero dangling refs. SI tables were embedded to satisfy source checks. Canonical figure helper cannot import unavailable src; canonical compile input gate rejects a system texmf.cnf symlink between approved system trees. Independent schema/render checks are being prepared; these environment issues will not be hidden.\n- next_action: Complete rendered-page, reference and source-line checks, finalize reproducibility and provenance, rerun grounding; leave required conceptual image for synchronous orchestrator rendering.\n\n### 2026-11-17T14:10:53.832334+00:00 | step=final | scope=paper\n- summary: paper.tex and claims_grounding.json are consistent with grounded inputs; gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=The final manuscript preserves the evaluated scope and adds no new mechanism-level recommendation.\n- evidence: Authored and compiled a nine-page main body with references and substantive embedded supplement; mapped 102 claims and 25 consulted references; three quantitative composite figures plus orchestrator-owned overview placeholder are registered. Canonical section/citation, claim-grounding and rendered-SI checks pass; scientific-evidence verification has zero dangling links. Independent