caled absolute residuals; a source must support the unit-scale specialization before being used for that construction. Existing general split-conformal and adaptive scalar-interval references do not by themselves establish this specific comparator attribution. Reuse an existing bibliography entry if its actual content supplies the needed support; do not imply that the exact simplex-intersected implementation originated in the source.\\\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \\\"query\\\": \\\"multivariate conformal prediction simultaneous prediction intervals coordinatewise Bonferroni joint maximum residual calibration\\\",\n+      \\\"category\\\": \\\"baseline_comparator\\\",\n+      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"For the same paragraph at paper.tex:85, especially the anchor 'The latter is a simultaneous-coordinate baseline', seek direct context for alpha/K coordinatewise conformal intervals and their distinction from a jointly calibrated maximum-residual region. Prefer a source explicitly discussing both constructions; complementary sources may support them separately. This query targets multiplicity and simultaneous vector coverage rather than another generic adaptive-interval search. Cite only the constructions verified in the source, without asserting comparative efficiency, a new validity theorem, or publication of the exact compositional implementation. One source covering both queries is sufficient.\\\"\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \\\"forbidden_query_patterns\\\": [\n+    \\\"Any attempt to identify, infer, search for, or retrieve the prohibited paper, its authors, title, results, mirrors, summaries, code, or pages citing, discussing, or reproducing it\\\",\n+    \\\"Broad joint-topic discovery searches such as conformal prediction compositional outputs evaluation or conformal uncertainty simplex benchmark that could directly target the prohibited study; search separate foundational neighborhoods instead\\\",\n+    \\\"Citation-network expansion, cited-by searches, related-paper recommendations, or unrestricted survey crawling around potentially prohibited material\\\",\n+    \\\"Queries treating classification labels, softmax probabilities, latent categorical probabilities, CIFAR-10, image classifiers, or text/topic datasets as evaluated responses or benchmarks\\\",\n+    \\\"Queries presenting Hellinger, Euclidean-versus-ILR, additive-logratio, Mondrian, boundary-face mass, structural zeros, neural architectures, or architecture transfer as implemented comparisons\\\",\n+    \\\"Queries presenting weighted conformal, jackknife+, conformalized quantile regression, credal sets, clustered conformal, or multivalid prediction as evaluated methods\\\",\n+    \\\"Generic optimization, benchmarking, scientific-method, paper-writing, campaign chronology, or internal scoring and acceptance terminology without a specific grounded scientific purpose\\\",\n+    \\\"Queries asserting distribution-free conditional validity, shift robustness, universal efficiency superiority, or a causal selector improvement not established by the recorded evidence\\\",\n+    \\\"New searches for already-supported general calibration, adaptive scaling, compositional geometry, conditional or shifted validity, statistical ingredients, or benchmark context; the only authorized enrichment target is the existing multivariate comparator paragraph\\\"\n+  ],\n+  \\\"failure_reason\\\": \\\"\\\"\n+}\n*** Add File: postdraft_reference_query_plan.md\n+# Post-draft incremental reference query plan\n+\n+**Status:** ready\n+\n+## Topic and scope\n+\n+Supply direct multivariate conformal context for the existing joint simplex-coordinate maximum-residual and coordinatewise Bonferroni comparators in the study of scale-adaptive split-conformal regions for observed positive continuous compositions. This is an attribution and positioning task, not a literature refresh, a new experiment, or a missing validity proof.\n+\n+The primary drivers, \"'`draft_reference_gaps.md` and `draft_reference_gaps.json`, identify exactly one medium-priority target: `multivariate-rectangular-comparators`. The bounded destination is **Prediction Regions and Evaluation Targets → Compared region constructions**, `paper.tex:85`, anchored by “The latter is a simultaneous-coordinate baseline”.\n+\n+**Budget: two focused queries, aiming for one or two citations total.** The single gap and its two explicit search topics justify fewer than the default three to six queries. Both queries address the same paragraph, but distinguish joint-score construction from coordinatewise multiplicity correction. A single source supporting both constructions can close the gap; do not seek one new reference per query.\n+\n+## Grounding and existing coverage\n+\n+Reviewed the two gap artifacts first, then `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, `references_provenance.json`, `global_memory_summary.md`, and the schema and exclusions in `reference_query_plan.json`. The memory summary supplies qualitative context only, not numerical or bibliographic evidence.\n+\n+The existing bibliography contains 25 entries with provenance. General conformal foundations and regression calibration are already represented by `Vovk2005`, `Papadopoulos2002`, `Lei2018`, and `Angelopoulos2021`; the draft also cites adaptive scalar-interval work including `Romano2019` and `Sesia2020`. These are context, not evidence that the specific multivariate comparator families have already been directly attributed. The provenance records for these entries report abstract, overview, or metadata-level reading rather than verified full-text support for this paragraph. Do not infer that these sources lack the relevant material: downstream, reuse an existing entry if its actual content supports the needed construction before adding a redundant reference. Deduplicate any new candidate against existing titles and DOI/arXiv identifiers.\n+\n+## Queries\n+\n+### 1. Joint maximum-residual rectangles\n+\n+- **Query:** `multivariate split conformal rectangular prediction regions maximum standardized absolute residual score`\n+- **Category:** `baseline_comparator`\n+- **Rationale:** At `paper.tex:85`, directly contextualize the vector-response rectangular region obtained by jointly calibrating a maximum absolute residual score. A standardized-score formulation is relevant family context, but the implemented **Simplex max** comparator is unscaled; verify that the source supports the unit-scale specialization. This fills a specific comparator-attribution gap rather than repeating a general split-conformal or adaptive scalar-interval search.\n+- **Acceptance boundary:** Support must concern simultaneous vector-response regions and joint maximum-score calibration. Cite only verified general constructions; do not imply that the exact simplex-intersected implementation was taken from the source. Reuse an existing suitable bibliography entry where possible.\n+\n+### 2. Coordinatewise Bonferroni versus joint calibration\n+\n+- **Query:** `multivariate conformal prediction simultaneous prediction intervals coordinatewise Bonferroni joint maximum residual calibration`\n+- **Category:** `baseline_comparator`\n+- **Rationale:** At the same paragraph and its simultaneous-coordinate anchor, directly contextualize separate coordinatewise calibration at miscoverage `alpha/K` and distinguish it from a jointly calibrated maximum-residual threshold. This is the multiplicity/simultaneous-coverage aspect of the gap, not another search for adaptive marginal intervals.\n+- **Acceptance boundary:** Prefer a source explicitly discussing both constructions; complementary sources can support them separately. Do not claim an efficiency ordering, a new validity theorem, or publication of the exact compositional implementation. One source satisfying both queries is enough.\n+\n+## Context deliberately not queried\n+\n+- **Core methods:** Independent split calibration and maximum standardized ILR residuals already have general conformal and compositional support; no additional core-method search is planned.\n+- **Comparators:** The targets are exactly the joint simplex-coordinate maximum-absolute-residual region and separate coordinatewise Bonferroni intervals intersected with the simplex. The scaled and unscaled ILR comparisons remain unchanged.\n+- **Benchmarks:** The existing synthetic logistic-normal and Dirichlet IID and shifted evaluations supply context only. No dataset or benchmark-context gap was identified.\n+- **Architectural shifts and result-driven themes:** No incremental queries are justified. Citation additions cannot establish selector-only effects, conditional or shifted validity, uniform efficiency superiority, or results on unevaluated data or geometries.\n+\n+## Exclusions and stopping rule\n+\n+Preserve the original plan'\"'s exclusions: no attempt to identify or retrieve the prohibited study or derivative material; no broad joint compositional-conformal discovery, citation-network expansion, cited-by traversal, related-paper recommendations, or unrestricted survey crawling. Keep the searches in the separate multivariate-conformal neighborhood without compositional benchmark terms. Do not introduce classification, image/text datasets, latent probabilities, alternative geometries, structural zeros, neural architectures, or architecture transfer as evaluated settings. Do not present weighted conformal, jackknife+, quantile-regression conformal, credal, clustered, or multivalid methods as implemented comparisons. Avoid generic optimization, benchmarking, writing, chronology, or internal scoring searches. Do not search to support unsupported conditional-validity, shift-robustness, superiority, or selector-causality claims.\n+\n+Do not reopen already-supported calibration, adaptive scaling, compositional geometry, conditional/shifted validity, statistical ingredients, or benchmark topics. Stop once one or two verified sources, including suitable existing entries, directly support both comparator families. The only intended downstream manuscript edit is a short context sentence or citation clause in the existing paragraph; no new experimental method, section, or manuscript reframing is needed. An unsuccessful targeted pass should leave this positioning gap unresolved rather than expand the search or imply support.\n+\n+No reference retrieval or network lookup was performed. The manuscript, bibliography, and provenance are unchanged.\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\npython - <<'PY'\nimport json\nimport re\nfrom pathlib import Path\noriginal=json.loads(Path('reference_query_plan.json').read_text())\nplan=json.loads(Path('postdraft_reference_query_plan.json').read_text())\ngaps=json.loads(Path('draft_reference_gaps.json').read_text())\nmemo=Path('postdraft_reference_query_plan.md').read_text()\nassert set(plan)==set(original), 'Top-level schema keys differ'\nfor key in original:\n assert type(plan[key]) is type(original[key]), key\nassert plan['status']=='ready' and plan['central_topic'].strip()\nassert plan['failure_reason']==''\nassert gaps['status']=='ready' and len(gaps['rewrite_targets'])==1\nassert len(plan['queries'])==2\nallowed={'core_method','baseline_comparator','benchmark_context','architectural_shift','result_driven_theme'}\nfor query in plan['queries']:\n assert set(query)==set(original['queries'][0])\n assert all(isinstance(query[key],str) and query[key].strip() for key in ('query','rationale'))\n assert query['category'] in allowed\n assert query['category']=='baseline_comparator'\n assert query['query'] in memo\n assert query['query'] not in {item['query'] for item in original['queries']}\n assert 'paper.tex:85' in query['rationale']\nassert len({query['query'] for query in plan['queries']})==2\nassert set(original['forbidden_query_patterns']) <= set(plan['forbidden_query_patterns'])\ntarget=gaps['rewrite_targets'][0]\nassert target['target_id'] in memo and target['anchor_text'] in memo\nassert target['anchor_text'] in Path('paper.tex').read_text()\nassert all(key in memo for key in ('one or two citations','No reference retrieval','one medium-priority target'))\nbib_keys=set(re.findall(r'@\\\\w+\\\\s*\\\\{\\\\s*([\"'^,'\"\\\\s]+)',Path('references.bib').read_text()))\nprovenance=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nassert len(bib_keys)==25 and provenance['count']==25\nassert bib_keys=={record['citation_key'] for record in provenance['references']}\nprint('PASS: schema matches; two distinct comparator queries map to one grounded target; memo mirrors decisions; original exclusions preserved; bibliography/provenance counts agree.')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
